{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:18191732 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:18191732 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"18191732","source":"MED","pmid":"18191732","doi":"10.1016/j.jacc.2007.09.036","title":"Effect of intravenous iron sucrose on exercise tolerance in anemic and nonanemic patients with symptomatic chronic heart failure and iron deficiency FERRIC-HF: a randomized, controlled, observer-blinded trial.","authorString":"Okonko DO, Grzeslo A, Witkowski T, Mandal AK, Slater RM, Roughton M, Foldes G, Thum T, Majda J, Banasiak W, Missouris CG, Poole-Wilson PA, Anker SD, Ponikowski P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Okonko DO","lastName":"Okonko","firstName":"Darlington O","initials":"DO","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3752-4540"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Cardiology, NHLI, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom. d.okonko@imperial.ac.uk"}]}},{"fullName":"Grzeslo A","lastName":"Grzeslo","firstName":"Agnieszka","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Witkowski T","lastName":"Witkowski","firstName":"Tomasz","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9383-4578"}},{"fullName":"Mandal AK","lastName":"Mandal","firstName":"Amit K J","initials":"AK"},{"fullName":"Slater RM","lastName":"Slater","firstName":"Robert M","initials":"RM"},{"fullName":"Roughton M","lastName":"Roughton","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Foldes G","lastName":"Foldes","firstName":"Gabor","initials":"G"},{"fullName":"Thum T","lastName":"Thum","firstName":"Thomas","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4360-1511"}},{"fullName":"Majda J","lastName":"Majda","firstName":"Jacek","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Banasiak W","lastName":"Banasiak","firstName":"Waldemar","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Missouris CG","lastName":"Missouris","firstName":"Constantinos G","initials":"CG"},{"fullName":"Poole-Wilson PA","lastName":"Poole-Wilson","firstName":"Philip A","initials":"PA"},{"fullName":"Anker SD","lastName":"Anker","firstName":"Stefan D","initials":"SD"},{"fullName":"Ponikowski P","lastName":"Ponikowski","firstName":"Piotr","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3391-7064"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3391-7064"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3752-4540"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9383-4578"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4360-1511"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"51","journalIssueId":1500341,"dateOfPublication":"2008 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2008,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Am Coll Cardiol","title":"Journal of the American College of Cardiology","essn":"1558-3597","issn":"0735-1097","nlmid":"8301365","isoabbreviation":"J Am Coll Cardiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2008-01-01"},"pubYear":"2008","pageInfo":"103-112","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>We tested the hypothesis that intravenous iron improves exercise tolerance in anemic and nonanemic patients with symptomatic chronic heart failure (CHF) and iron deficiency.<h4>Background</h4>Anemia is common in heart failure. Iron metabolism is disturbed, and administration of iron might improve both symptoms and exercise tolerance.<h4>Methods</h4>We randomized 35 patients with CHF (age 64 +/- 13 years, peak oxygen consumption [pVO2] 14.0 +/- 2.7 ml/kg/min) to 16 weeks of intravenous iron (200 mg weekly until ferritin >500 ng/ml, 200 mg monthly thereafter) or no treatment in a 2:1 ratio. Ferritin was required to be <100 ng/ml or ferritin 100 to 300 ng/ml with transferrin saturation <20%. Patients were stratified according to hemoglobin levels (<12.5 g/dl [anemic group] vs. 12.5 to 14.5 g/dl [nonanemic group]). The observer-blinded primary end point was the change in absolute pVO2.<h4>Results</h4>The difference (95% confidence interval [CI]) in the mean changes from baseline to end of study between the iron and control groups was 273 (151 to 396) ng/ml for ferritin (p < 0.0001), 0.1 (-0.8 to 0.9) g/dl for hemoglobin (p = 0.9), 96 (-12 to 205) ml/min for absolute pVO2 (p = 0.08), 2.2 (0.5 to 4.0) ml/kg/min for pVO2/kg (p = 0.01), 60 (-6 to 126) s for treadmill exercise duration (p = 0.08), -0.6 (-0.9 to -0.2) for New York Heart Association (NYHA) functional class (p = 0.007), and 1.7 (0.7 to 2.6) for patient global assessment (p = 0.002). In anemic patients (n = 18), the difference (95% CI) was 204 (31 to 378) ml/min for absolute pVO2 (p = 0.02), and 3.9 (1.1 to 6.8) ml/kg/min for pVO2/kg (p = 0.01). In nonanemic patients, NYHA functional class improved (p = 0.06). Adverse events were similar.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Intravenous iron loading improved exercise capacity and symptoms in patients with CHF and evidence of abnormal iron metabolism. Benefits were more evident in anemic patients. (Effect of Intravenous Ferrous Sucrose on Exercise Capacity in Chronic Heart Failure; http://www.clinicaltrials.gov/ct/show/NCT00125996; NCT00125996).","affiliation":"Clinical Cardiology, NHLI, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom. d.okonko@imperial.ac.uk","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Anemia, Iron-Deficiency","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MO","qualifierName":"mortality"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chronic Disease"},{"majorTopic_